{
  "requirement": "After one valid judgment, the harness MUST write the subject text, submitted\ndetermination and rationale, and recorded inputs using the existing evidence\nschema. {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`\nBy default, it MUST write the subject's evidence file. With `--stdout`, it MUST\nemit a single evidence JSON object followed by a newline to stdout and MUST NOT\ncreate or change evidence files or directories. All three outcomes MUST be\nwritten to the selected destination as completed assessments; their validation\nstatus MUST follow {cite}`requirements.ci.determination`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the persistence requirement is met.** Every obligation has marked implementation code and test coverage. I found two small marking questions, which I did not count as gaps.\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Record contents.** `record` (src/authoring/mod.rs:787) builds the object from the subject text under `requirement` or `example` (via `kind.noun()`), plus `determination`, `rationale`, `references` and `inputs`. This is the existing schema. Citation reviews get their own shape, covered by `requirements.references.citation_review`. `assess` (:804) produces the record only after `converse` returns one accepted submission, so it is written after one valid judgment.\n- **All three outcomes.** `Judgment::to_value` (src/judgment.rs:43) maps true and false to booleans and FIXME to `{\"FIXME\":[…]}`. All three go through the same write path, so each is stored as a completed assessment. The `usage` record is finished with outcome `completed`. How each outcome is validated is left to `requirements.ci.determination`, which this requirement only cites.\n- **Default destination.** `configured_assessment` (:378) creates the parent directory and writes the JSON to `AuthoringSubject::evidence_path`, which is `evidence/<name>.json` for a definition. Batch mode does the same through `write_record` (batch.rs:287), writing the same pending record via a temporary file and rename, with `PendingAssessment::persisted` finishing usage.\n- **`--stdout`.** The `EvidenceOutput::Stdout` branch writes exactly one pretty-printed JSON object plus `\\n` to stdout and flushes. It never calls `create_dir_all` or writes a file. `--all` conflicts with `--stdout` in clap, and `main.rs` maps the flag to `EvidenceOutput`. The usage log goes to the state directory, which `usage_state_root` forces to be outside the project.\n- **Write failure.** `evidence_written` is set only after a successful write. If the write fails, the error propagates and the usage record keeps the accepted judgment.\n\n**Marking questions**\n- `PendingAssessment` (mod.rs:118) carries only `{cite}` to this requirement. It is a data carrier, and the behavior is in the marked `persisted` and `write_record`.\n- The `--stdout`-to-`EvidenceOutput` mapping in `main.rs` is marked only under `invocation`. I treated that as flag plumbing.\n\n**Tests**\n- `stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files` (authoring_cli.rs:1075) covers:\n  - true, false and FIXME;\n  - an existing evidence file left byte-identical;\n  - no `evidence` directory created when it was absent;\n  - a single JSON object ending in `\\n`;\n  - the requirement text, rationale, and input forms (requirements, definition, and outline inputs);\n  - the usage log recording the stdout output.\n- `false_is_a_completed_assessment` (:605) checks the default file write. It also checks that the outline inputs hold selectors and hashes with no source text, and that `validate_repository` rejects the false determination while reporting its rationale.\n- `verbose_single_author_reports_only_false_rationale_without_source_inputs` (:1034) checks stored determination and rationale for true and false in file mode.\n- The batch tests in tests/authoring_batch.rs cover file persistence for true, false and FIXME (the mixed-judgments test checks `{\"FIXME\":[\"#123\"]}`) and examples. They also check that a failed assessment writes no evidence (`evidence_written` false) without affecting the other assessments' evidence.\n- `failed_evidence_write_keeps_the_accepted_judgment_in_usage` (:1508) covers the write-failure case.\n- The live Bedrock test also exercises `--stdout` against a real model.\n\nNo gaps are acknowledged by FIXME comments, and I found none that need one.",
  "references": "c51ee997790716abb90d24ddd3e340402f10480b9cc3cac01f4dc7eed2034142",
